From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Schwartz <matthew.schwartz@linux.dev>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 17:23:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] =?UTF-8?q?render:=20add=20Snapdragon=E2=84=A2=20Game?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20Super=20Resolution=201=20upscaling?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Steam Frame's Sharp option is remapped to FSR, which costs 925 us of
GPU per 720p to 1080p frame in NieR Replicant, 600 in EASU and 325 in
RCAS. Snapdragon™ Game Super Resolution 1 is a single twelve tap pass
sized for this GPU class, but the reference rebuilds green alone and
adds that delta to every channel, which tints color edges and lets a
lone dark texel in a sprite be outvoted by its neighbors where EASU
keeps it.

Take filter value 5 for SGSR, which is what Steam Frame already writes
for Sharp, so the remap goes away. Reconstruct every channel with the
weights green computes and fade the outlier term in with tap distance,
without the reference edge gain since RCAS sharpens. One pixel per
thread, and red and blue gathered only once green's weights exist,
each measured faster than EASU's layout. With RCAS that is 680 us in
NieR, 26 percent under FSR, and 530 against 900 in Dave the Diver,
with lone texels and color edges holding up against FSR in both. SGSR
reads SDR RGB only, so HDR keeps the FSR that Sharp ran before and
YCbCr scales linearly.
